Sign in as an Energy Analyst.
View the analyst dashboard.
Sign out and sign in as a Data Contributor.
Upload a CSV file containing sample residential energy records.
Validate and save the records to PostgreSQL.
Return to the analyst dashboard.
Show updated consumption analytics and area comparisons.
Open the Leaflet map and explore the available geographic data.
Show a high-consumption observation and explain the rule that flagged it.
Sign in as an Energy Planner and create a planning action.
Sign in as an Administrator and show system health and user management.
Export an actual report.

For the demonstration, use a small, clearly labelled dataset containing several urban areas, observation dates, consumption values, and consistent units. Make sure the dataset includes enough variation to demonstrate comparisons and the high-consumption rule.

Do not claim the dataset represents real utility measurements unless it genuinely does.

One important technical distinction

The four logins can have four different visual identities and dashboards while sharing one authentication backend. That is the simpler, safer design.

Similarly, you can build genuine database-backed analytics and an interactive real-world map without connecting to live electricity meters. Real-time electricity readings require a genuine data source; the website must not invent them.

The goal is to finish with a small, attractive application that genuinely works—not twenty complicated screens that look complete but fail when someone clicks them.